WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.820
When Bash sees a command, it does not always start a new external program.

2
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:10.040
It may run a shell builtin, call a function, or locate an external executable.

3
00:00:10.140 --> 00:00:20.220
That distinction matters because functions and builtins run inside the shell context, while external commands run as separate processes with their own argument list and runtime context.

4
00:00:20.500 --> 00:00:24.340
For an external command, Bash prepares an invocation.

5
00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:34.520
The process receives an executable, a structured list of arguments, environment values, a current working directory, and standard input, output, and error streams.

6
00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:40.780
Thinking in those terms is safer than imagining a vague command string that somehow runs as typed.

7
00:00:41.060 --> 00:00:44.180
The environment is inherited unless the script changes it.

8
00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:47.960
The current directory is inherited unless the script changes it.

9
00:00:48.060 --> 00:00:52.860
Standard streams are inherited or redirected according to the script.

10
00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:55.200
Each of those pieces can affect behavior.

11
00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:02.980
A tool may look up configuration in the environment, read from standard input, write to standard output, or report problems on standard error.

12
00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.220
The command eventually returns an exit status.

13
00:01:06.320 --> 00:01:09.920
That status is the process saying how it believes the work ended.

14
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:16.340
Scripts that ignore important exit status values risk building later decisions on false assumptions.

15
00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:23.640
A failed command, missing dependency, denied permission, or partial result should not silently become a successful workflow.

16
00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.480
The safer mental model is executable plus arguments plus context plus result.

17
00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:41.380
When a Bash script is written around that model, reviewers can reason about what program runs, what values it receives, what assumptions shape it, and how the script reacts afterward.
